  "title": "Carrying the Dead",
  "data": "It is also part of the tradition that those who have offered the Prayer for the Deceased, especially the one who led it, should stay in their places until the bier is lifted.\nAccording to a reported tradition, when the Prayer for the Deceased is finished, these words should be said:\nرَبَّنَا اٰتِنَا فِى الدُّنْيَا حَسَنَةً وَفِى الْاٰخِرَةِ حَسَنَةً وَقِنَا عَذَابَ النَّارِ\nRABBANAA AATINAA FID DUNYAA HASANATAN WA FIL AAKHIRATI HASANATAN WA QINAA A'DHAABAN NAAR\nOur Lord! grant us good in this world and good in the Hereafter, and save us from the chastisement of the Fire. (2:201)\nImam al-Sadiq (a.s.) is reported to have said that it is recommended to inform the brethren-in-faith of the death of their brother, so that they will attend the funeral, offer prayers over his body, and ask Almighty Allah to forgive him. When they do so, both they and the dead person are rewarded.\nAccording to another acceptably reported tradition, Imam al-Sadiq (a.s.) said, “When a faithful believer is placed in the grave, a caller calls out to him: Verily, the first of your gifts is Paradise, and the first gift of those who followed you (in your funeral) is forgiveness.”\nAccording to another tradition, Imam al-Sadiq (a.s.) said, “The first gift a faithful believer receives in his grave is that all those who followed his funeral are forgiven.”\nAccording to another tradition, Imam al-Sadiq (a.s.) said, “Whoever follows the funeral of a faithful believer to the grave, Almighty Allah will order seventy angels to escort him on the Day of Resurrection and ask Allah to forgive him, from the grave to the place of Judgment.”\nThe Imam (a.s.) is also reported to have said, “Whoever takes part in carrying the coffin of a faithful believer, Almighty Allah will forgive twenty-five of his major sins. If he carries the four sides of the coffin (in turn), all his sins will be forgiven.”\nCarrying the Dead Person and the Coffin\nThe coffin should be carried by four men. It is better for one who escorts the deceased to begin by carrying it at the side of the deceased's right hand, which is on the left side of the coffin; then at the side of the right leg. Then, going round to the back of the coffin, he should carry the side of the left leg on his left shoulder, and then the side of the left hand on his left shoulder. If he wants to go round again, he should not pass in front of the coffin but turn from behind it, beginning again with the side of the right hand in the same way. This way, however, differs from the instruction of most scholars, who say that one should begin with the right side of the front of the coffin, then the right side at the back, then the left side at the back, and then the left side at the front.\nHowever, the first way in fact matches the most validly reported traditions. Best of all, both ways may be followed.\nIt is also preferable to walk behind the coffin or at its two sides rather than in front of it. Yet the majority of traditions apparently hold that it is better to walk in front of the coffin of a faithful believer, and that it is disapproved to walk in front of the coffin of one who followed another sect, for the angels receive that coffin with chastisement. Finally, it is disliked to follow a funeral riding."
